<Layout title="About and sources · Left Behind" description="About the Left Behind space exploration archive and its sources.">
  <div class="about">
    <h1>About this project</h1>
    <p><strong>Left Behind</strong> is an independent educational archive about humanity's material footprint in space. It documents spacecraft that still work, machines that went silent, landers and rovers left on other worlds, retired stations, and objects that re-entered or crashed.</p>
    <p>The atlas is a visual index, not a live orbital tracker. Marker positions are illustrative and not to scale. Mission dates, outcomes and status descriptions are based on the agency records linked in each mission file.</p>
    <h2>What the archive includes</h2>
    <ul class="sources">
      <li>Operational spacecraft and observatories that are still returning science.</li>
      <li>Silent, lost, damaged or decommissioned spacecraft.</li>
      <li>Rovers, landers, probes and equipment left on the Moon, Mars, Venus and elsewhere.</li>
      <li>Space stations and satellites that re-entered Earth's atmosphere.</li>
      <li>Mission failures, impact events and the engineering lessons they left behind.</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>Research sources</h2>
    <ul class="sources">
      <li><a href="https://science.nasa.gov/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NASA Science</a> — mission histories and science results.</li>
      <li><a href="https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NASA Jet Propulsion Laboratory</a> — spacecraft mission records and engineering updates.</li>
      <li><a href="https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive</a> — historical spacecraft records.</li>
      <li><a href="https://www.isro.gov.in/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Indian Space Research Organisation (ISRO)</a> — Indian spacecraft and planetary mission records.</li>
      <li><a href="https://www.esa.int/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">European Space Agency (ESA)</a> — European planetary and Earth-orbit missions.</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>Important note</h2>
    <div class="note"><p>This is an independent educational project and is not affiliated with or endorsed by NASA, ISRO, ESA or any other space agency. Mission status can change; check the linked agency source for the latest operational information. Image credits are shown alongside images when supplied.</p></div>
  </div>
</Layout>
